s=json.loads("\"\\\"Perturbation Effects on Word Lengths in Three-Reflection Symmetric Presentations of Dihedral Groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.19216)\\\\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Title: Perturbation Effects on Word Lengths in Three-Reflection Symmetric Presentations of Dihedral Groups\\\\nTitle: Perturbation Effects on Word Lengths in Three-Reflection Symmetric Presentations of Dihedral Groups\\\\nAuthors: Michael Allocca, Max Trimmer\\\\nDate: Tue Jun 24 00:50:00 2025\\\\n\\\\nWe investigate the properties of word lengths of elements from a three-reflection symmetric generating set of the dihedral group $D_n$. Specifically, we provide the upper bound $\\\\\\\\lambda_1(D_n,S) \\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\lfloor\\\\\\\\frac{n}{2}\\\\\\\\rfloor + 1$ for a quantity $\\\\\\\\lambda_1$ defined in arXiv:1104.5044, which measures the stability of a finitely presented group under perturbations in the words corresponding to certain elements with respect to specific presentations. This quantity has been of recent interest due to its role in the application of group theory to computational genomics, and we aim to introduce techniques in additive combinatorics to its discourse.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\\\nISOTONIAN ALGEBRAS　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　99 (https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/07AE28F37C3292F136F603414E2EE529/S0027763017000046a.pdf/isotonian_algebras.pdf)\\\\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.6 years ago; Crawled: 4.6 years ago; ISOTONIAN ALGEBRAS　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　99 ... **CONJECTURE 5.7.** *Let \\\\\\\\(P,Q\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\mathcal{P}\\\\\\\\), and assume that \\\\\\\\(Q\\\\\\\\) does not contain any induced poset cycle of length greater than 4. Then \\\\\\\\(J_{P,Q}\\\\\\\\) is generated by quadratic binomials.\\\\nISOTONIAN ALGEBRAS　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　99\\\\n\\\\nBased on Theorem 5.5, Corollary 5.6 and experimental evidence we propose\\\\n\\\\n**CONJECTURE 5.7.** *Let \\\\\\\\(P,Q\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\mathcal{P}\\\\\\\\), and assume that \\\\\\\\(Q\\\\\\\\) does not contain any induced poset cycle of length greater than 4. Then \\\\\\\\(J_{P,Q}\\\\\\\\) is generated by quadratic binomials.*\\\\n\\\\nThe poset \\\\\\\\(Q\\\\\\\\) given in Figure 3 is a poset cycle. As expected by our conjecture, \\\\\\\\(J_{[2],Q}\\\\\\\\) is not generated by quadrics. Actually, \\\\\\\\(J_{[2],Q}\\\\\\\\) does not even possess a quadratic Gröbner basis with respect to the reverse lexicographic term order induced by the natural order of the variables.\\\\n\\\\n## REFERENCES\\\\n\\\\n[1] A. Aramova, J. Herzog and T. Hibi, *Finite lattices and lexicographic Gröbner bases*, European J. Combin. **21** (2000), 431–439.\\\\n\\\\n[2] W. Bruns and J. Herzog, *Cohen–Macaulay Rings*, Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics **39**, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1996, Revised edition.\\\\n\\\\n[3] W. Bruns, B. Ichim and C. Söger, NORMALIZ. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\\\nISOTONIAN ALGEBRAS (https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/nagoya-mathematical-journal/article/isotonian-algebras/07AE28F37C3292F136F603414E2EE529)\\\\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; Based on these results and on computational evidence we are lead to conjecture that all isotonian algebras are normal Cohen–Macaulay domains. ... In Theorem 5.5 we show that if Image$P$ is a chain and Image$Q$ is a rooted or co-rooted poset, then Image$J_{P,Q}$ has a quadratic (and hence also a squarefree) Gröbner basis with respect to the reverse lexicographic order induced by a canonical labeling of the variables. ... On the other hand, we conjecture that Image$J_{P,Q}$ is quadratically generated if and only if Image$Q$ does not contain any induced poset cycle of length greater than 4.\\\\nThen Image$K[P,Q]$ is normal if and only if Image$K[P^{\\\\\\\\prime },Q]$ is normal. Based on these results and on computational evidence we are lead to conjecture that all isotonian algebras are normal Cohen–Macaulay domains. One way to prove this conjecture in general would be to show that there exists a term order such that the initial ideal of the defining ideal Image$J_{P,Q}$ of Image$K[P,Q]$ is squarefree. By a theorem of Sturmfels [Reference Sturmfels17, Chapter 8] this would imply that Image$K[P,Q]$ is normal, and by a theorem of Hochster [Reference Hochster13, Theorem 1] this in turn implies Cohen–Macaulayness. In Theorem 5.5 we show that if Image$P$ is a chain and Image$Q$ is a rooted or co-rooted poset, then Image$J_{P,Q}$ has a quadratic (and hence also a squarefree) Gröbner basis with respect to the reverse lexicographic order induced by a canonical labeling of the variables. In general, Image$J_{P,Q}$ may contain generators of arbitrarily high degree. This happens if Image$Q$ contains as an induced subposet, what we call a poset cycle. On the other hand, we conjecture that Image$J_{P,Q}$ is quadratically generated if and only if Image$Q$ does not contain any induced poset cycle of length greater than 4.\\\\n\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\\\n2  J.L. GROSS, T. MANSOUR, T.W. TUCKER, AND D.G.L. WANG (https://www.cs.columbia.edu/~gross/research/LocalLC-GMTW-EuroJC-2016.pdf)\\\\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.9 years ago; We formulate Conjecture 2.2, a topological conjecture, called the *Local Log-Concavity Conjecture*. ... Section 4 gives an elementary proof of local log-concavity for vertices of degree four or less.\\\\n\\\\nSection 2 introduces the concepts of the *local genus polynomial* and *local log-concavity*. We formulate Conjecture 2.2, a topological conjecture, called the *Local Log-Concavity Conjecture*. This conjecture has a purely combinatorial equivalent form, given in Section 3 as Conjecture 3.1. Section 4 gives an elementary proof of local log-concavity for vertices of degree four or less. Section 5 examines how to use Jackson’s formula [Ja87] in explorations of the log-concavity of local genus distributions. Section 6 shows how the concept of *synchronicity*, introduced previously by the present authors [GMTW15], provides a plausible path to a predominantly combinatorial proof of the LCGD Conjecture.\\\\n\\\\nThis study evolved partially from the general idea of seeking a simplifying effect when one vertex of a graph is removed or added.\\\\n\\\\n## 2. LOCAL LOG-CONCAVITY\\\\n\\\\nA **partial rotation at a vertex** \\\\\\\\(v\\\\\\\\) is a cyclic sequence, each of whose elements is either\\\\n\\\\n(1) an edge-end that is incident at \\\\\\\\(v\\\\\\\\), or  \\\\n(2) an ellipsis (\\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\cdots\\\\\\\\)).\\\\n\\\\nAlthough there may be more than one ellipsis, none of the edge-ends may occur more than once. A partial rotation whose only element is an ellipsis is said to be **empty**. A partial rotation with no ellipsis is said to be **full**.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\\\nThe quadratic-binomial generation conjecture for isotonian toric ideals | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/331981/the-quadratic-binomial-generation-conjecture-for-isotonian-t)\\\\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # The quadratic-binomial generation conjecture for isotonian toric ideals ... If $Q$Q does not contain any induced poset cycle of length greater than $4$4, then $J_{P,Q}$J P,Q​ is generated by quadratic binomials.\\\\n\\\\n# The quadratic-binomial generation conjecture for isotonian toric ideals\\\\n\\\\nAbout 11 years old · traced to 2015\\\\n\\\\nLet $P$P and $Q$Q be finite posets, and let $J_{P,Q}$J P,Q​ be the defining toric ideal of the isotonian algebra $K[P,Q]$K[P,Q]. A poset cycle of length $\\\\\\\\ell$ℓ is a subposet with vertices $a_1,\\\\\\\\ldots,a_\\\\\\\\ell,b_1,\\\\\\\\ldots,b_\\\\\\\\ell$a 1​,…,a ℓ​,b 1​,…,b ℓ​ and covering relations\\\\n\\\\n$$a_i<b_i,\\\\\\\\qquad a_i<b_{i+1}\\\\\\\\quad (1\\\\\\\\leq i\\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\ell),$$a i​<b i​,a i​<b i+1​(1≤i≤ℓ),\\\\n\\\\nwhere $b_{\\\\\\\\ell+1}=b_1$b ℓ+1​=b 1​. An induced poset cycle is one induced by the relevant vertices.\\\\n\\\\nQuadratic-binomial generation conjecture. If $Q$Q does not contain any induced poset cycle of length greater than $4$4, then $J_{P,Q}$J P,Q​ is generated by quadratic binomials.\\\\n\\\\nThis is the sufficient-condition direction of the paper’s broader quadratic-generation conjecture and is supported by the stated Gröbner-basis results and experiments. Its general validity remains open.\\\\n\\\\nReferences\\\\n\\\\n## Primary source\\\\n\\\\nMina Bigdeli, Jürgen Herzog, Takayuki Hibi, Ayesha Asloob Qureshi and Akihiro Shikama, “Isotonian Algebras”, arXiv:1512.01973 (2015).\\\\n\\\\ngroebner-basesposetstoric-ideals\\\\n\\\\n0 0\\\\n\\\\nPosted 1 month ago\\\\n\\\\n## Progress summary\\\\n\\\\nRefresh Never refreshed\\\\n\\\\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\\\\n\\\\n## Solutions 0\\\\n\\\\n[Button: Post a solution]\\\\n\\\\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\\\\n\\\\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\\\nPerturbation Effects on Word Lengths in Three-Reflection Symmetric Presentations of Dihedral Groups (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392980260_Perturbation_Effects_on_Word_Lengths_in_Three-Reflection_Symmetric_Presentations_of_Dihedral_Groups)\\\\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; equal to the maximum word length required to write a reﬂection in D n using elements of\\\\nmodulo p, then |W l| ≥ min(3 l, p)with respect to S for l > 0\\\\n\\\\nProof. We ﬁrst note that F(S)−F(S) = {0, a, b, a −b, b−a, −a, −b}. Since we have assumed\\\\n\\\\nthese are all distinct m